---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ProjectHeader from "../components/ProjectHeader.astro";
import NextProject from "../components/NextProject.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import Stats from "../components/Stats.astro";
import { bySlug } from "../data/projects";

const project = bySlug("trusst");

/** The six-step cup molding sequence; the caption number is the step. */
const molding = [
  { src: "/img/trusst-mold.jpg", alt: "Aluminum molds for the inner foam layer", width: 1600, height: 1200, n: "1", text: "Mold for the inner layer." },
  { src: "/img/trusst-inner-molding.jpg", alt: "Inner foam layer in the press", width: 1600, height: 1200, n: "2", text: "Inner layer being molded." },
  { src: "/img/trusst-place-structure.jpg", alt: "Worker placing the structure into the molded indentations", width: 1600, height: 950, n: "3", text: "Structure placed in the formed indentations." },
  { src: "/img/trusst-second-layer.jpg", alt: "Second foam layer being molded over the structure", width: 1600, height: 945, n: "4", text: "Second foam layer molded over the first layer and structure." },
  { src: "/img/trusst-molded-cup.jpg", alt: "Fully molded foam cup", width: 1600, height: 975, n: "5", text: "Fully molded cup." },
  { src: "/img/trusst-trim.jpg", alt: "Worker cutting molded cups to size", width: 1600, height: 945, n: "6", text: "Cup cut to the size marked in the mold." },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={project.pageTitle} current="work">
<ProjectHeader project={project} />

<Section title="The product">
<div class="row">
<div class="prose"><p>The support structure is flexible, cantilevered and articulating. It is injection molded, then over-molded into a bra cup. The finished cups are assembled into the garment with specialty wicking fabrics to manage moisture.</p></div>
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-illustration.jpg"
  alt="Cutaway illustration of a Trusst bra showing the support structure inside the cup"
  width={1500} height={1226}
  caption="Cutaway showing the structure inside the cup."
/>
</div>
<div class="figs three">
<Figure src="/img/trusst-model-1.jpg" alt="Model wearing a black Trusst bra with lace trim" width={977} height={1200} />
<Figure src="/img/trusst-model-2.jpg" alt="Model wearing a black lace Trusst bra" width={800} height={1000} />
<Figure src="/img/trusst-model-3.jpg" alt="Model wearing a nude Trusst bra" width={800} height={1000} />
</div>
</Section>

<Section title="Step 1 · Support structure design">
<div class="prose"><p>The structure had to support the breast from underneath and carry the weight to the core of the body, while staying flexible and comfortable as the body moves. We settled on an articulated, cantilevered design and refined it by iterating quickly through 3D printing before investing in injection molding tooling.</p>
<p>We also tested several plastics, balancing flexibility against the heat resistance needed for washing and drying and overall durability. We chose a nylon.</p></div>
<div class="figs two">
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-3d-printing.jpg"
  alt="Support structure printing on a 3D printer bed"
  width={750} height={726}
  caption="Sizes 1–3 printed together. Size 4 printed one side at a time to fit the small bed."
/>
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-pp-prints.jpg"
  alt="Close-up of 3D printed support structures in PP"
  width={1600} height={1200}
  caption="3D prints of structure sizes 1, 2 and 3 in PP."
/>
</div>
</Section>

<Section title="Step 2 · Manufacturability">
<Stats items={[
  { v: "8", k: "Cup volumes" },
  { v: "4", k: "Structure sizes" },
  { v: "1 cm", k: "Trim allowance at each end" },
  { v: "32F–42G", k: "Bra size range" },
]} />
<div class="row" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
<div class="prose"><h3>Support structure</h3><p>The design had to cover eight cup volumes with as few structures as possible to keep cost and complexity down. Four structure sizes cover all eight volumes, and I created the injection molding tooling for final production.</p>
<p>Seam allowances meant the structure needed adjustable length so it could be trimmed per bra size. The nylon supports have 1 cm of variance at each end. We worked with our cup factory on a custom die-cut tool to trim them precisely.</p></div>
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-structure.jpg"
  alt="Molded support structure with the size and logo marked"
  width={1600} height={1047}
  caption="Production structure. Size and logo are molded in."
/>
</div>
<div class="figs three">
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-structure-cutmarks.jpg"
  alt="Structure size 2 with red cut marks"
  width={1600} height={979}
  caption="Structure 2 with cut marks."
/>
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-die-cut.jpg"
  alt="Die-cut tool positioned on a support structure"
  width={918} height={912}
  caption="Die-cut tool placed on the structure."
/>
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-cup-seam.jpg"
  alt="Foam cup with the structure embedded and a 1 cm seam allowance marked"
  width={460} height={522}
  caption="Cup with structure embedded and seam allowance."
/>
</div>
<div class="prose" style="margin-top:3rem"><h3>Molding the cups</h3><p>We also developed a process with the cup factory to place the structure accurately inside the foam cups.</p></div>
<div class="figs three">
{molding.map((m) => (
  <Figure src={m.src} alt={m.alt} width={m.width} height={m.height}>
    <Fragment slot="caption"><b>{m.n}</b> {m.text}</Fragment>
  </Figure>
))}
</div>
</Section>

<Section title="Step 3 · Cut and sew">
<div class="row">
<div class="prose"><p>Once the cups arrive at the sewing factory they are sewn into bras. The rigid structure inside makes sewing more physically demanding than a traditional underwire bra.</p>
<p>The lace edges also meant pattern layout and markers had to be chosen carefully, and production workers were trained to place patterns for minimum waste.</p></div>
<div class="figs two" style="margin-top:0">
<Figure src="/img/trusst-sew-1.jpg" alt="Sewing a cup on an industrial machine" width={1365} height={1600} />
<Figure src="/img/trusst-sew-2.jpg" alt="Sewing a structured cup" width={1600} height={1350} />
<Figure src="/img/trusst-lace-1.jpg" alt="Placing paper patterns on lace" width={1365} height={1600} />
<Figure src="/img/trusst-lace-2.jpg" alt="Laying out cup patterns on black lace" width={1600} height={1350} />
</div></div>
<div style="margin-top:3rem">
<Figure
  src="/img/trusst-techpack.jpg"
  alt="Bra tech pack page showing construction callouts on a technical flat"
  width={1397} height={1025} pad
  caption="A bra tech pack I created for overseas manufacturers, style TL3001BK."
/>
</div>
</Section>

<NextProject slot="next" from={project.slug} />
</BaseLayout>
